this is bloomberg quick take i'm  scarlett fu  the white house has officially confirmed  that the u.s is staging a diplomatic  boycott against the upcoming 2022 winter  olympics in beijing white house press  secretary jen saki said the athletes of  team usa have quote our full support but  there will be no diplomatic or official  representation at the games so what does  this mean why does it matter let's bring  in dr jules boykov he is professor of  politics and government at pacific  university in oregon he's also author of  four books on the olympic games  including power games a political  history of the olympics professor boykov  thank you so much for speaking with us  so u.s athletes would still be able to  attend and compete in the olympics what  exactly would a diplomatic boycott then  accomplish  so a diplomatic boycott would be a  strong signal to both china and the  international community that the u.s  government will stand up against crimes  against humanity that is what human  rights organizations have been calling  what's happening in xinjiang province at  the moment and so it'll be powerfully  symbolic to stand up against that kind  of thing happening it'll be interesting  to see also whether this  makes other countries join and the  allies of the united states might also  get on board with this so that'll be  interesting to see in the days that come  and to be clear we made a certain that  this is not an athlete boycott  what would turn this into an economic  boycott  well you'd have to have a lot more  economic power to have an economic  boycott of china in general the united  states is incredibly reliant on china  china owns a lot of the u.s debt and so  that would be an entirely different ball  game the reason why they're going with a  diplomatic boycott is because it speaks  against the possibility of sports  washing this is a term we see a lot in  the news these days sports washing is  essentially when a country uses a sports  mega event like the olympics or the  world cup of soccer to try to launder  its reputation on the world stage and by  not sending diplomats to the beijing  olympics that means that the united  states is trying to deprive china of the  possibility of sports washing all those  grin and grip photo ops that are typical  within olympic games won't be happening  at least not with diplomats from the  united states and possibly their allies  okay so it would deprive china of that  photo finish moment where there's lots  of pictures a lot of pageantry because  the people that it wants to impress  simply won't be there attending the  actual games china's take on all this  professor is that beijing apparently had  no plan of inviting u.s politicians to  the games so how can there be a boycott  of an event that they were not invited  to attend  yeah it's going to be really interesting  to follow the back and forth chinese  officials have also said that the united  states doing a diplomatic boycott  violates the spirit of the olympic  charter well a lot of things violate the  spirit of the olympic charter including  carrying out human rights abuses against  your own population so the war of words  will definitely be continuing for the  next couple months in the lead up to the  beijing games right right right you  talked about the crimes against humanity  and other human rights abuses  specifically what we've seen in xinjiang  but none of that is particularly new it  feels in fact like old news simply  because it's been with us for a long  time now  isn't the nba and what's been happening  with the chinese tennis star feng shui  more relevant or at least more immediate  catalysts how much do you think that  played into the us's decision  i think the feng shui situation is  really important in a number of ways  first of all it really raised the  profile of what's happening in china  right now  second of all it was a harbinger  possibly of what could happen to an  athlete should they have the temerity to  speak out against what's happening in  china against any official and maybe  even against the international olympic  committee and the olympics in responding  to the situation with the tennis player  the international olympic committee very  much took the side of chinese  authorities sending a signal to athletes  that in reality when it comes to the  clutch and the prime olympic moment the  international olympic committee might  not have your back as an athlete and so  that would be pretty jarring if you were  thinking about going to beijing as an  olympic athlete and perhaps making a  stand about something that mattered to  you politically the ioc is signaled that  it really doesn't have your back despite  its slogans like athletes come first  when it comes to punctuality they  believe everything that the chinese  authorities have said they haven't asked  for verification they haven't asked for  safe patch out of the country and those  are all big signals to athletes who are  headed to beijing yeah so it's kind of a  warning to any of the olympians who do  go there and and perhaps want to take  advantage of that moment to to really  make clear their point of view on what's  going on in china china has also vowed  to take counter measures we don't know  what that means yet and of course  they've kept it vague the u.s we know  will host the 2028 olympic games in la  would it be too far out for china to say  something about that right  now well it'll be interesting to see  that's one possibility sort of a tit for  tat talking just olympics only but china  has a lot of possibilities at its  disposal you know it's the united states  is quite reliant on china economically  at this point and so we'll see what they  decide to do let's not forget that the  united states is no stranger to  humanitarian crises i'm coming to you  from the west coast of the united states  where homelessness is a humanitarian  crisis in plain sight then there's  guantanamo there's family separation at  the border and so i expect all of those  topics to come into the conversation  when china pushes back against the  government for the united states saying  things to beijing right now yeah  accusing the u.s of crimes against  humanity and human rights abuses there  are plenty of examples there if you  consider what we just discussed now well  we know the olympics are supposed to be  apolitical but come on that's never been  the case from the beginning of time  give us a few of the most recent  examples in which it's clear just how  political the olympics have become  well the olympics were political from  day one when a plucky baron from france  baron pierre to kuberton started the  olympics he brought together powerful  political people from around the world  to create the international olympic  committee he was quite straightforward  about the fact that he needed political  support for his games it's been  political all the way through 1936 when  berlin hosted the olympics and the nazis  created the olympic torch run as a way  of not only getting the olympic spirit  throughout germany but also as a way to  sort of scout out possible invasions  that happen in the wake of those 1936  olympics fast forward to more current  times and look at the 2014 olympics in  sochi where you had  anti-lgbtq laws passed immediately ahead  of those olympics and a lot of athletes  wanting to speak out about that pretty  much every single olympics have  political elements to it and if somebody  tells you otherwise there's a good  chance that they're milking the olympic  machine with both hands meaning they're  making their money off of the olympic  games anybody who looks at the olympics  objectively will see immediately that  they're throwing with the political  that's a great great synopsis there of  the history of how the olympics have  have always been political so looking  ahead professor boykov what do you see  as the next step how else might china  position itself before the 2022 games  well for starters next step we'll see  who joins the united states in a  diplomatic boycott second we have to  keep an eye on whether that eventually  swerves into the possibility of an  athlete boycott that still remains to be  seen that would be a whole different  kettle of two expect that is that likely  i do not believe that is likely i think  a lot of people inside of the olympic  sphere were very much scarred by the  double boycotts of 1980 and 1984. many  of those athletes have been outspoken  and having to live with an asterisk by  their name where it says olympian  asterisk because they didn't actually  get to participate and so i think their  pushback has been so vociferous that i  wouldn't expect an athlete boycott but  hey if things are difficult to predict  we wouldn't have predicted coronavirus  we wouldn't have necessarily predicted a  lot of what's happened in the last  couple of years so anything's really  possible in the next couple months all  right professor jules boykov thank you  so much pacific university professor of  government and politics we'll be  checking in with you as we get closer to  the 2022 olympic games in beijing this  is bloomberg quick take thanks for  joining us  [Music]

